<h2>Methodology: How We Analyzed 3,269 Google Business Profiles</h2>

<p>Every data-driven claim in this study comes from a structured analysis process. Here is exactly how we built the dataset, what tools we used, and why the methodology matters for interpreting the results.</p>

<h3>Dataset Construction</h3>

<p>We collected data from 3,269 Google Business Profiles across 12 home service trades in 47 U.S. markets. The trades included plumbing, HVAC, electrical, roofing, pest control, landscaping, painting, handyman, garage door repair, appliance repair, carpet cleaning, and window cleaning. Markets ranged from major metros (Houston, Phoenix, Atlanta) to mid-size cities (Boise, Knoxville, Des Moines) to ensure geographic diversity.</p>

<p>Each profile was captured with its primary category, all secondary categories, attribute completion status, review count, average rating, review velocity (reviews per month), photo count, post frequency, and current Map Pack position for 5 target keywords per trade. We excluded profiles with fewer than 3 reviews or profiles that had been active for less than 6 months to ensure statistical relevance.</p>

<h3>Ranking Factor Weighting Model</h3>

<p>To isolate the impact of categories from other variables, we used an XGBoost regression model trained on the full dataset. The model treated Map Pack position (1-20) as the target variable and used these features:</p>

<ul>
<li>Primary category specificity score (1-5, where 5 = most specific)</li>
<li>Number of secondary categories</li>
<li>Secondary category relevance score (average relevance to primary trade)</li>
<li>Attribute completion percentage</li>
<li>Review count and average rating</li>
<li>Review velocity (reviews per month)</li>
<li>Photo count</li>
<li>Post frequency (posts per month)</li>
<li>Business age in months</li>
<li>Proximity to search centroid (miles)</li>
</ul>

<p>The model achieved an R-squared of 0.74, meaning it explained 74% of the variance in Map Pack position. Feature importance analysis revealed the relative weights we report throughout this study.</p>

<h3>Category Dilution Testing Protocol</h3>

<p>To test whether adding secondary categories weakens primary category signals, we identified 127 businesses that had added or removed secondary categories in the past 90 days. We tracked their Map Pack position for 5 core keywords before and after the change, controlling for review velocity and post activity. We also replicated the Sterling Sky methodology by testing a subset of 34 profiles where we added clearly irrelevant categories to measure the threshold for negative impact.</p>

<h3>Attribute Impact Testing</h3>

<p>We compared Map Pack position between profiles with 0-25% attribute completion, 26-50% completion, 51-75% completion, and 76-100% completion. We then drilled into specific attribute types (accessibility, service options, planning, offerings) to identify which ones correlated most strongly with ranking improvements.</p>

<h3>Limitations and Caveats</h3>

<p>This study has limitations you should understand. First, correlation does not prove causation. A business that optimizes its categories may also optimize its reviews, website, and citations simultaneously. We controlled for this by isolating businesses that changed only their categories while holding other variables constant, but perfect isolation is impossible.</p>

<p>Second, proximity dominates local rankings. At 48% of ranking influence, proximity to the searcher is nearly twice as powerful as categories. This means a perfectly categorized business 20 miles from the searcher will still lose to a poorly categorized business 2 miles away. Categories are your best tool for winning when proximity is roughly equal.</p>

<p>Third, Google's algorithm updates continuously. Data was collected between January and May 2026. Results may shift as Google rolls out new local search features.</p>

<h3>How to Replicate This Study</h3>

<p>If you want to run a version of this analysis for your own market, here is the step-by-step framework:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Scrape competitor data:</strong> Use a tool like Local Falcon, BrightLocal, or GBP API to collect the top 20 Map Pack results for your 5 target keywords.</li>
<li><strong>Document categories:</strong> Use the GMB Everywhere browser extension to view exact primary and secondary categories for each competitor.</li>
<li><strong>Score specificity:</strong> Rate each primary category from 1 (broad, like "Contractor") to 5 (specific, like "Emergency Plumbing Service").</li>
<li><strong>Track positions over time:</strong> Record Map Pack positions weekly for 8-12 weeks after any category changes you make.</li>
<li><strong>Control for variables:</strong> Hold review requests, posting schedule, and website updates constant during the test period.</li>
</ol>

