<h2>Key Findings: What the Data Actually Says About GBP Categories</h2>

<p>The numbers tell a clear story. Here are the 7 findings that every home service contractor needs to understand.</p>

<h3>Finding #1: Primary Category Accounts for 21% of Ranking Influence</h3>

<p>Our XGBoost model assigned a 21% feature importance score to industry type (categories). Only proximity (48%) scored higher. This means your primary category is the second-most powerful ranking factor overall and the most powerful factor you can directly control.</p>

<p>Here is what that looks like in practice. A plumbing business in Atlanta using "Contractor" as its primary category ranked an average of 9.3 positions lower than a competitor using "Plumber" for the keyword "plumber near me." When the same business switched to "Plumber," its average position improved by 6.8 spots within 21 days.</p>

<p>The specificity multiplier matters too. Businesses using the most specific available category (e.g., "Tankless Water Heater Repair Service" instead of "Plumber") saw an additional 2.1-position improvement on average when that specific category matched the search query.</p>

<h3>Finding #2: Category Dilution Is a Myth for Relevant Categories</h3>

<p>Of the 127 businesses we tracked that added secondary categories, 94% saw either no change or a positive change in their primary keyword rankings. Only 6% saw any decline, and in every case of decline, the added categories were loosely related or clearly irrelevant.</p>

<p>Sterling Sky's Joy Hawkins summarized this perfectly: "99 problems but GBP category dilution (still) ain't one." Our data confirms her finding. Adding relevant secondary categories does not dilute your primary signal. It expands your visibility into related searches.</p>

<p>The threshold for negative impact appears to be around 60% relevance. When secondary categories had a relevance score below 0.6 (on a 0-1 scale), we started seeing ranking volatility. Above 0.6, the effect was neutral to positive.</p>

<h3>Finding #3: The Optimal Secondary Category Count Is 4-6</h3>

<p>Google allows up to 9 secondary categories. Our analysis found a clear sweet spot:</p>

<div class="cost-table">
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Secondary Category Count</th><th>Average Map Pack Position</th><th>Keyword Coverage</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>0-1</td><td>8.4</td><td>Low</td></tr>
<tr><td>2-3</td><td>6.7</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>4-6</td><td>4.9</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>7-9</td><td>5.8</td><td>Very High</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Businesses with 4-6 relevant secondary categories ranked an average of 1.9 positions higher than those with 2-3. But businesses with 7-9 categories saw their average position drop back to 5.8, suggesting that overstuffing triggers a relevance penalty. The data points to a Goldilocks zone: enough categories to cover your services, not so many that Google questions your focus.</p>

<h3>Finding #4: Changing Your Primary Category Causes Immediate Volatility</h3>

<p>When businesses changed their primary category, we observed a predictable pattern. Rankings for the old primary keyword dropped within 48 hours. Rankings for the new primary keyword started improving within 72 hours. Full stabilization took 14-21 days.</p>

<p>Of the 23 businesses we tracked that changed primary categories, 78% were in a better position after 21 days than before the change. The 22% that underperformed had all switched to broader, less specific categories. The lesson: only change your primary category if you are moving to a more specific, more accurate option.</p>

<h3>Finding #5: Attribute Completion Correlates with 2.3-Position Improvement</h3>

<p>Profiles with 76-100% attribute completion ranked an average of 2.3 positions higher than profiles with 0-25% completion. The effect was strongest for service-based attributes like "Online appointments," "Onsite services," and "Free estimates." Identity-based attributes ("Women-led," "Veteran-owned") showed a smaller but still positive correlation of 0.8 positions on average.</p>

<p>The mechanism is clear. When a user searches for "plumber who offers free estimates," Google filters results by the "Free estimates" attribute. Businesses without that attribute do not appear, regardless of their reviews or proximity.</p>

<h3>Finding #6: Seasonal Category Switching Works for HVAC</h3>

<p>We tracked 14 HVAC businesses that switched their primary category seasonally: "Air Conditioning Repair Service" in summer, "Heating Contractor" in winter. These businesses saw an average 3.2-position improvement for seasonal keywords compared to HVAC businesses that kept "HVAC Contractor" as their primary year-round.</p>

<p>The effect was most pronounced in markets with distinct seasons (Minneapolis, Denver, Boston) and less noticeable in mild climates (San Diego, Phoenix). For trades without strong seasonality (plumbing, electrical), switching categories caused more volatility than benefit.</p>

<h3>Finding #7: Category + Review Keyword Synergy Is Undervalued</h3>

<p>Businesses whose primary category matched the most common keyword in their reviews saw an additional 1.4-position boost. For example, a roofing contractor with "Roofing Contractor" as its primary category and 60% of reviews mentioning "roof repair" outperformed a competitor with the same category but generic reviews by 1.4 positions.</p>

<p>This suggests Google cross-references category signals with review content to verify relevance. Your category tells Google what you do. Your reviews confirm it.</p>

