<h3>The One Thing Most Contractors Get Wrong</h3>

<p>The most common mistake we see is set-and-forget syndrome. A contractor sets their categories when they create their profile and never touches them again. Meanwhile, Google adds new categories, competitors optimize theirs, and search behavior shifts.</p>

<p>Our data shows that businesses that audit their categories quarterly rank an average of 2.7 positions higher than businesses that never audit. The time investment is 30 minutes per quarter. The ranking impact is measurable within weeks.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Google Business Profile Categories</h2>

<h3>What is the most important Google Business Profile category ranking factor?</h3>
<p>Your primary category is the single most important category-related ranking factor. It accounts for approximately 21% of your local pack ranking influence, second only to proximity at 48%. The primary category tells Google what your core business is, which searches to show you for, and which features to enable on your profile. Choose the most specific category that matches your main revenue source.</p>

<h3>How many secondary categories should I add to my GBP?</h3>
<p>Our analysis of 3,269 businesses found the optimal number is 4-6 relevant secondary categories. Businesses with 4-6 secondary categories ranked an average of 1.9 positions higher than those with 2-3. However, businesses with 7-9 categories saw their rankings drop back down, suggesting that overstuffing triggers a relevance penalty. Quality and relevance matter more than quantity.</p>

<h3>Does adding secondary categories dilute my primary category ranking?</h3>
<p>No, not if the secondary categories are relevant. A 2025 study by Sterling Sky and our independent analysis both confirmed that adding relevant secondary categories does not hurt your primary category rankings. In fact, 94% of businesses that added relevant secondary categories saw either no change or improved visibility. The only time categories cause problems is when they are irrelevant to your actual business.</p>

<h3>Can I change my Google Business Profile primary category?</h3>
<p>Yes, you can change your primary category at any time through your GBP dashboard. However, expect 14-21 days of ranking volatility. Your old primary keyword rankings may drop within 48 hours, while new keyword rankings improve within 72 hours. Only change your primary category if you are moving to a more specific, more accurate option. Avoid changing during your busiest season.</p>

<h3>What are the best GBP categories for plumbers?</h3>
<p>For most plumbers, "Plumber" is the best primary category. Use "Emergency Plumbing Service" only if emergency work represents 70%+ of revenue. The highest-impact secondary categories are Drain Cleaning Service, Water Heater Installation/Repair, Pipe Repair Service, Sewer Service, and Septic System Service. Avoid broad categories like "Contractor" or "Home Services."</p>

<h3>Should HVAC contractors switch categories seasonally?</h3>
<p>Yes, if you are in a market with distinct seasons. Our data showed HVAC businesses that switched from "HVAC Contractor" to "Air Conditioning Repair Service" in summer and "Heating Contractor" in winter saw an average 3.2-position improvement for seasonal keywords. The effect was strongest in markets like Minneapolis, Denver, and Boston. In mild climates, the benefit is smaller and may not justify the volatility.</p>

<h3>What are service area business categories and how do they differ?</h3>
<p>Service area business (SAB) categories are the same categories available to all businesses, but SABs face a unique challenge. Without a storefront address providing proximity signals, your category relevance becomes even more critical. For SABs, we recommend being more specific with your primary category (e.g., "Drain Cleaning Service" instead of "Plumber") to maximize relevance signals that compensate for the lack of address-based proximity.</p>

<h3>Do Google Business Profile attributes affect rankings?</h3>
<p>Yes. Attributes have evolved from nice-to-have to moderate-impact ranking factors. Profiles with 76-100% attribute completion ranked an average of 2.3 positions higher than profiles with 0-25% completion. Service option attributes like "Free estimates" and "Online appointments" showed the strongest correlation. Attributes also determine whether you appear in filtered searches like "wheelchair accessible plumber near me."</p>

<h3>How often should I update my GBP categories?</h3>
<p>We recommend auditing your categories quarterly. Google adds new categories regularly (3 new categories were added in the past 30 days alone). Your business also evolves, adding or removing services over time. Businesses that audit their categories quarterly rank an average of 2.7 positions higher than those that never audit. Each audit takes approximately 30 minutes.</p>

<h3>What happens if I choose the wrong primary category?</h3>
<p>Choosing the wrong primary category is one of the most expensive mistakes in local SEO. A plumbing business using "Contractor" instead of "Plumber" ranked an average of 9.3 positions lower for "plumber near me." The wrong category means Google does not understand what you do, so it does not show you for the searches that matter. If you suspect your category is wrong, change it, but expect 2-3 weeks of volatility before rankings stabilize.</p>

<h3>Can I use the same categories as my competitors?</h3>
<p>Yes, and you should if they are accurate for your business. Using the GMB Everywhere browser extension to see your competitors' categories is a standard competitive research tactic. However, do not copy categories blindly. If a competitor has "Restaurant" as a secondary category and you are a plumbing company, adding it will hurt you. Only copy categories that are genuinely relevant to your services.</p>

<h3>What are predefined services in Google Business Profile?</h3>
<p>Predefined services are structured service options that Google provides based on your primary category. Instead of manually typing "drain cleaning," you can select "Drain Cleaning" from a dropdown. Predefined services match exact search queries with higher confidence than custom text. Our analysis found that profiles using predefined services for core offerings ranked 1.1 positions higher than profiles using only custom services.</p>

<h3>Do identity attributes like "Veteran-owned" help rankings?</h3>
<p>Identity attributes showed a smaller but consistent positive correlation of 0.8 positions on average. Google surfaces these attributes in dedicated filters and has reported that searches for identity-specific businesses increased over 600% in recent years. Only add identity attributes that are true and verifiable. False claims can result in profile suspension.</p>

<h3>How do I know if my categories are working?</h3>
<p>Track these three metrics before and after any category change: Map Pack position for your top 5 keywords (use Local Falcon or BrightLocal), GBP insights for profile views and actions (calls, direction requests, website clicks), and organic traffic to your website from GBP (check Google Analytics 4 with UTM parameters). Give each change 21 days to stabilize before drawing conclusions.</p>

<h3>Should I create multiple GBP listings for different services?</h3>
<p>No, unless they are genuinely separate business entities with different names, phone numbers, and addresses. Creating multiple listings for the same business at the same location violates Google's guidelines and can result in suspension. Instead, use secondary categories and the Services section to show your full range of offerings within a single profile.</p>

<h2>Conclusion: Your Categories Are a Strategic Weapon</h2>

<p>The data from this study is unambiguous. Your Google Business Profile category is not a formality. It is a strategic weapon that accounts for 21% of your local ranking influence. The businesses that treat category selection as an ongoing optimization, not a one-time setup task, are capturing visibility their competitors do not even know exists.</p>

<p>Here are the three actions to take this week:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Audit your current categories.</strong> Compare your primary and secondary categories against the trade-specific recommendations in this study. If your primary category is not the most specific accurate option available, plan a change.</li>
<li><strong>Fill every relevant attribute.</strong> Go through your GBP dashboard and enable every attribute that applies to your business. Pay special attention to Service Options and Offerings, which showed the highest ranking correlation.</li>
<li><strong>Set a quarterly reminder.</strong> Google adds new categories regularly. Your competitors are not standing still. Schedule 30 minutes every 90 days to review and update your categories, attributes, and services.</li>
</ol>

<p>The 58% of local businesses that do zero local SEO optimization have already conceded the field. The 42% that do optimize are mostly doing the basics: claiming their profile, adding a few photos, and hoping for the best. The contractors who win are the ones who treat every element of their GBP, especially categories, as a lever they can pull to move rankings.</p>

<p>Your primary category is the strongest lever you control. Pull it.</p>

